WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:12.040
Session security risks usually come from weak identifiers, weak storage, long exposure windows, or incomplete lifecycle handling. Predictable session identifiers can undermine the entire authentication model.

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:21.220
Excessive session lifetimes increase the value of a stale or exposed session. Improper logout can leave an old session usable longer than the user expects.

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:30.160
Insecure storage can expose tokens to places the application did not intend. Session fixation and session hijacking are important concepts to understand defensively.

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:42.140
Session fixation involves an attempt to make a victim use a known session identifier. Session hijacking is unauthorized use of another user?s active session. This course does not teach techniques for either behavior.

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:54.280
The defensive lesson is that applications should rotate identifiers at authentication, protect cookies and tokens, validate sessions server-side, expire sessions appropriately, and watch for unusual session activity.

00:00:54.500 --> 00:01:05.780
Usability and security often need balance. A very short timeout may frustrate users and increase risky workarounds. A very long timeout may leave accounts exposed on shared or lost devices.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.800
Concurrent sessions may be convenient, but they also require visibility and control.

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:23.020
The best design is risk-based: administrative actions, financial workflows, account recovery, sensitive data access, and privilege changes usually deserve stronger session controls than ordinary low-risk navigation.
